<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|<tuple|tmdoc|english>>

<\body>
  <tmdoc-title|Starting <TeXmacs> up>

  How you start <TeXmacs> depends on your system:

  <\itemize>
    <item>on <name|Windows>, choose <TeXmacs> in the start menu or
    double-click on its icon on the desktop;

    <item>on <name|macOS>, open <TeXmacs> from the <verbatim|Applications>
    folder, from the Launchpad or from the Dock;

    <item>on <name|Linux> and other <name|Unix> systems, choose <TeXmacs> in
    the menu of applications of your desktop, or type

    <\shell-code>
      texmacs &
    </shell-code>

    in a terminal.
  </itemize>

  You may also open an existing document directly, by double-clicking on a
  file whose name ends with <verbatim|.tm>, or by giving its name on the
  command line, as in <verbatim|texmacs mydocument.tm>.

  The first time that you start <TeXmacs>, the program inspects your system:
  it looks for the fonts which are installed and for the other programs which
  it can work with (such as <name|Python>, <name|Maxima> or <name|Gnuplot>). This
  may take a little while. Afterwards, a window with an empty document
  appears on your screen, ready for typing. It is described in detail in the
  next section.

  If <TeXmacs> does not start, or if it complains about missing components,
  the messages which it prints may help you to track down the problem. You
  may also consult the web site of <TeXmacs>

  <\verbatim>
    \ \ \ \ <hlink|https://www.texmacs.org|https://www.texmacs.org>
  </verbatim>

  where you will find the installation instructions for each system, the
  answers to the frequently asked questions and the addresses of the mailing
  lists and of the forum, where the developers and other users of <TeXmacs>
  will be glad to help you.

